<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Greco-Roman world</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Greco-Roman, Graeco-Roman world</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Greco-Roman world refers to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> civilisation shaped by Greek culture and Roman rule in classical antiquity. Its trade networks, languages and ideas extended to neighbouring regions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a> was closely connected to it through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> commerce, using Greek in inscriptions and coinage and adopting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, which linked Aksum to the wider Mediterranean.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/greco-roman-world.jpg" alt="Greco-Roman world" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Pierre Dalbéra from Paris, France · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%27Olympieion_(Ath%C3%A8nes)_(30776483926).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…a functioning polity appears in the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea, a Greek merchant's guide of the mid-first century CE, which names the metropolis of the Axomites and describes the local ruler Zoskales as versed in Greek letters, early evidence of the region's engagement with the <b>Graeco-Roman world</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the same years the Scythians, pushed on by the Yuezhi, overran Bactria, the last Greco-Bactrian king Heliocles was probably killed, and the Indo-Greeks were cut off from the <b>Graeco-Roman world</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The cult of Matar Kubeleya spread from central Anatolia to Pisidia and then to the <b>Greco-Roman world</b> as Kybele, Cybele, with Attis beside her.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-phrygia">Kingdom of Phrygia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
